1
00:00:01,958 --> 00:00:04,874
[up-tempo jazz playing]

2
00:00:04,961 --> 00:00:07,877
[indistinct conversations]

3
00:00:11,098 --> 00:00:12,360
Let's do it.

4
00:00:12,447 --> 00:00:14,014
[clinking]

5
00:00:15,972 --> 00:00:18,583
- Thank you, Sadie.
- Mm-hmm.

6
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:23,023
My friends, I am so proud
and happy to welcome you

7
00:00:23,110 --> 00:00:25,460
to this celebration
of my son Xander

8
00:00:25,547 --> 00:00:28,767
the new C.E.O.
of Harrington Media.

9
00:00:28,854 --> 00:00:32,119
[male #1]
'Here, here.'

10
00:00:32,206 --> 00:00:35,078
Where is
the little cuss, anyway?

11
00:00:35,165 --> 00:00:37,994
Late for work already,
not a good sign.

12
00:00:38,081 --> 00:00:39,517
Elizabeth, text him,
would you?

13
00:00:39,604 --> 00:00:40,779
You got it.

14
00:00:40,866 --> 00:00:42,129
Um, I am sure my husband

15
00:00:42,216 --> 00:00:44,435
'has a very good excuse
for his..'

16
00:00:44,522 --> 00:00:46,394
'Ah, there you are, Xander.'

17
00:00:48,309 --> 00:00:49,701
[male #2]
'Bravo Xander.'

18
00:00:51,834 --> 00:00:53,966
[crowd applauding]

19
00:01:00,103 --> 00:01:01,539
[crowd gasping]

20
00:01:01,626 --> 00:01:03,063
[murmuring]

21
00:01:10,983 --> 00:01:12,594
[Cho]
That's what killed
Xander Harrington,

22
00:01:12,681 --> 00:01:13,986
'and this is where he was
attacked.'

23
00:01:14,074 --> 00:01:15,640
'Now, the blood trail
starts here'

24
00:01:15,727 --> 00:01:17,381
'right where the letter opener
was found.'

25
00:01:17,468 --> 00:01:19,470
The deputy coroner says it was
a single stab wound to the neck.

26
00:01:19,557 --> 00:01:21,994
Now the blow sends him
backwards.

27
00:01:22,082 --> 00:01:24,127
He slams his head,
goes down.

28
00:01:24,214 --> 00:01:27,435
He's dazed, slowly bleeding out
for a good 20 minutes or so,

29
00:01:27,522 --> 00:01:30,133
then somehow gathers himself up
and goes for help.

30
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:32,222
Coroner couldn't believe he even
made it out to the party.

31
00:01:32,309 --> 00:01:35,312
Party which was for him.
Guy just got a big promotion.

32
00:01:35,399 --> 00:01:37,009
Had a wife and kid.
How sad is that?

33
00:01:37,097 --> 00:01:38,533
Sad. Anything else?

34
00:01:38,620 --> 00:01:40,274
Well, according to the victim's
father, there was

35
00:01:40,361 --> 00:01:42,537
a confidential business file on
this desk that's gone missing.

36
00:01:42,624 --> 00:01:44,843
- Nothing else taken.
- What was the file about?

37
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:48,151
Plans for the "Daily Observer,"
the newspaper.

38
00:01:48,238 --> 00:01:50,501
'Debt restructuring,
cost cutting, layoffs.'

39
00:01:50,588 --> 00:01:52,155
Layoffs?

40
00:01:52,242 --> 00:01:53,939
Any "Observer" employees
invited to the party?

41
00:01:55,941 --> 00:01:58,379
So you're all reporters
for the, uh, "Observer."

42
00:01:58,466 --> 00:01:59,815
'That's right.'

43
00:01:59,902 --> 00:02:01,904
Xander Harrington--
man of the people, huh?

44
00:02:01,991 --> 00:02:04,602
Inviting
the ink-stained wretches.

45
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:06,256
Xander was a good guy.

46
00:02:06,343 --> 00:02:07,736
Did any of you hear
about the layoffs

47
00:02:07,823 --> 00:02:08,998
that were coming up soon?

48
00:02:09,085 --> 00:02:10,826
Layoffs?
What layoffs?

49
00:02:10,913 --> 00:02:13,829
Well, actually, there's,
uh, quite a long list.

50
00:02:13,916 --> 00:02:16,875
'Yeah. It appears that
no one is safe.'

51
00:02:16,962 --> 00:02:19,356
'Must be very scary in the
newspaper business these days.'

52
00:02:19,443 --> 00:02:21,402
That's a very lovely dress.

53
00:02:21,489 --> 00:02:23,099
'Great color.'

54
00:02:23,186 --> 00:02:24,622
Well, thank you.
[chuckles]

55
00:02:24,709 --> 00:02:26,015
I'm Heather Evans.

56
00:02:26,102 --> 00:02:27,843
Hello, Heather Evans.
You're very attractive.

57
00:02:27,930 --> 00:02:29,497
It's probably why
your name's not on that list.

58
00:02:29,584 --> 00:02:32,326
Good-looking women live
in a different world.

59
00:02:32,413 --> 00:02:34,458
Excuse me? What's this list
you keep mentioning?

60
00:02:34,545 --> 00:02:36,504
Oh, come on, Heather. The list
that was in the file

61
00:02:36,591 --> 00:02:38,897
that you took
off Xander Harrington's desk.

62
00:02:38,984 --> 00:02:40,682
Everyone here..
We mention layoffs,

63
00:02:40,769 --> 00:02:42,336
and everyone
here reacts except you

64
00:02:42,423 --> 00:02:44,512
'because you have read
the list.'

65
00:02:44,599 --> 00:02:46,122
Oh, that's ridiculous.

66
00:02:46,209 --> 00:02:48,124
I didn't go anywhere near
Xander's desk.

67
00:02:48,211 --> 00:02:50,082
Bravo. Very well played,

68
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:52,128
considering the stress
you must be feeling right now.

69
00:02:52,215 --> 00:02:53,999
You know, I was hoping
to dazzle you all

70
00:02:54,086 --> 00:02:57,960
with my uncanny detective
skills, but you force me..

71
00:02:58,047 --> 00:03:00,136
...you force me to just reveal
the humdrum facts

72
00:03:00,223 --> 00:03:02,356
and point out that you have
blood on your shoes, Heather.

73
00:03:07,230 --> 00:03:09,014
[Lisbon]
'Could you step out
of your shoe, ma'am?'

74
00:03:09,101 --> 00:03:11,103
"Stop the presses"?
That's the phrase, right?

75
00:03:11,191 --> 00:03:13,584
"Stop the presses."
Yeah. Got it.

76
00:03:14,890 --> 00:03:16,587
Always wanted to say that.

77
00:03:19,111 --> 00:03:22,027
[theme music]

78
00:03:31,167 --> 00:03:33,430
Xander Harrington caught you
snooping in his office

79
00:03:33,517 --> 00:03:35,302
and you killed him.

80
00:03:35,389 --> 00:03:37,347
Now maybe
you didn't mean to do it.

81
00:03:37,434 --> 00:03:38,914
You were surprised, scared--

82
00:03:39,001 --> 00:03:41,525
No. I didn't even see him.

83
00:03:41,612 --> 00:03:45,225
It was dark. No lights.

84
00:03:45,312 --> 00:03:47,183
Curtains were drawn.

85
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:49,925
I went to the desk

86
00:03:50,012 --> 00:03:51,579
I took the file..

87
00:03:51,666 --> 00:03:52,971
...and I ran.

88
00:03:55,191 --> 00:03:56,932
If I'd known he was..

89
00:03:58,673 --> 00:04:01,458
My God,
I would never have..

90
00:04:01,545 --> 00:04:03,504
How'd you know
that there was a list at all?

91
00:04:06,507 --> 00:04:09,118
Someone told you. Who?

92
00:04:09,205 --> 00:04:10,641
A confidential source.

93
00:04:10,728 --> 00:04:13,035
And you'll go to jail
to protect this source?

94
00:04:17,213 --> 00:04:20,912
Ed. Edward Harrington.
Xander's brother.

95
00:04:20,999 --> 00:04:23,263
He, um..

96
00:04:23,350 --> 00:04:26,135
He said there were gonna be
layoffs on Monday.

97
00:04:26,222 --> 00:04:27,702
'I just,
I just wanted to know

98
00:04:27,789 --> 00:04:29,660
'whether or not
I still had a job.'

99
00:04:32,228 --> 00:04:36,276
- It's a very good job.
- Not anymore, it's not.

100
00:04:36,363 --> 00:04:38,278
[Cho]
'Tell me exactly what
happened from the beginning.'

101
00:04:38,365 --> 00:04:40,323
Ma'am? Is there, uh..

102
00:04:40,410 --> 00:04:42,630
I-Is there something specific
that you wanted?

103
00:04:42,717 --> 00:04:46,677
No. No.
Just keeping apprised.

104
00:04:46,764 --> 00:04:48,331
Huh.

105
00:04:48,418 --> 00:04:49,941
Alex Harrington
is the owner

106
00:04:50,028 --> 00:04:52,248
of the seventh largest
media conglomerate in the world.

107
00:04:52,335 --> 00:04:53,858
Yes, ma'am.

108
00:04:53,945 --> 00:04:55,512
And you don't want
to disappoint a man

109
00:04:55,599 --> 00:04:57,384
who owns 200 TV stations
and a national newspaper.

110
00:04:57,471 --> 00:04:59,516
No, ma'am.

111
00:04:59,603 --> 00:05:01,301
Looks like Heather's cleared.

112
00:05:01,388 --> 00:05:03,433
Timeline puts her at the bar
at the time of the attack.

113
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:05,783
That's according to several male
guests who remember her well.

114
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:09,961
Figures. Only leaves 196 party
guests and 42 staff

115
00:05:10,048 --> 00:05:12,877
as potential suspects.
Start with the brother.

116
00:05:12,964 --> 00:05:14,575
Okay.

117
00:05:14,662 --> 00:05:16,664
Take Rigsby.

118
00:05:18,535 --> 00:05:19,971
Problem?

119
00:05:20,058 --> 00:05:22,234
Nope. Not at all.

120
00:05:22,322 --> 00:05:24,976
Ma'am? You're giving
my team assignments?

121
00:05:25,063 --> 00:05:28,328
I know. I'm sorry.
Should've been your call.

122
00:05:28,415 --> 00:05:30,808
I just want to make sure
those two can hack it.

123
00:05:30,895 --> 00:05:32,332
Fair enough.

124
00:05:39,948 --> 00:05:41,515
[Lisbon]
'The head of your
security said'

125
00:05:41,602 --> 00:05:43,995
'that the house is fully wired,
but the family asked'

126
00:05:44,082 --> 00:05:46,433
the cameras inside be
turned off? Why's that?

127
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:48,696
I take responsibility
for that.

128
00:05:48,783 --> 00:05:52,613
No, it's my fault. I'm Tara--
Alex's wife. This is Ella.

129
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:56,094
And I'm the one who had
the cameras disconnected.

130
00:05:56,181 --> 00:05:57,400
It's my fault.

131
00:05:57,487 --> 00:05:59,315
It's nobody's fault,
dear. Hush.

132
00:05:59,402 --> 00:06:01,012
Why'd you do it?

133
00:06:01,099 --> 00:06:03,928
Cameras and panic rooms
and armed guards--

134
00:06:04,015 --> 00:06:06,017
'it's a lot to take.'

135
00:06:06,104 --> 00:06:09,107
I hated feeling watched
in my own home.

136
00:06:09,194 --> 00:06:11,240
'Seems so silly now.'

137
00:06:11,327 --> 00:06:15,200
I'm sorry. How long until you
take that horrible stuff down?

138
00:06:15,287 --> 00:06:16,854
Forensics has promised
to release the crime scene

139
00:06:16,941 --> 00:06:19,161
as soon as possible. I am
so sorry for any inconvenience.

140
00:06:19,248 --> 00:06:20,423
[Ella whimpers]

141
00:06:20,510 --> 00:06:21,555
Excuse me.

142
00:06:23,034 --> 00:06:25,210
Uh, I'm too upset.

143
00:06:25,297 --> 00:06:28,083
Uh, m-m-may I?

144
00:06:28,170 --> 00:06:30,259
Thank you.

145
00:06:30,346 --> 00:06:32,174
[laughs]

146
00:06:32,261 --> 00:06:34,306
[Lisbon]
'You and Xander were close?'

147
00:06:34,394 --> 00:06:37,048
He and Sadie were
my only true friends.

148
00:06:37,135 --> 00:06:38,963
People seem more interested

149
00:06:39,050 --> 00:06:41,966
in befriending
your money and connections

150
00:06:42,053 --> 00:06:45,317
when you're married to
the C.E.O. Of Harrington media.

151
00:06:45,405 --> 00:06:49,017
[Alex]
Yes, I suppose
it can be difficult at times.

152
00:06:49,104 --> 00:06:51,889
By the way,
it'sformerC.E.O.

153
00:06:51,976 --> 00:06:54,196
- Xander forced me out.
- 'Really?'

154
00:06:54,283 --> 00:06:55,980
[Alex]
'Really.'

155
00:06:56,067 --> 00:07:00,289
'He convened the board,
secured the necessary votes.'

156
00:07:00,376 --> 00:07:02,247
Son staged a coup.

157
00:07:02,334 --> 00:07:03,988
Yes.

158
00:07:04,075 --> 00:07:07,078
You have any idea the guts
it takes to pull that off?

159
00:07:07,165 --> 00:07:11,953
Xander proved he could lead.
I've never been prouder of him.

160
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:14,869
Proud of, uh, Eddie, too,
my other son.

161
00:07:14,956 --> 00:07:16,697
[Patrick]
'Well, of course.'

162
00:07:16,784 --> 00:07:20,440
But Xander had
the strength to lead, the guts.

163
00:07:20,527 --> 00:07:22,746
That's important to you,
isn't it? Guts?

164
00:07:22,833 --> 00:07:27,229
Of course. Guts, heart.
What else is there?

165
00:07:27,316 --> 00:07:30,537
Spleen, liver, kidneys,
uh, genitalia.

166
00:07:30,624 --> 00:07:32,539
Lot of important stuff there.

167
00:07:32,626 --> 00:07:35,150
Are you being flippant
with me?

168
00:07:35,237 --> 00:07:37,195
'My son is dead.'

169
00:07:37,282 --> 00:07:38,893
Forgive me. I-I was.

170
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:41,025
Lost myself for a second.
Forgot where I was.

171
00:07:41,112 --> 00:07:43,463
But, you know, that-that's
a very beautiful painting.

172
00:07:43,550 --> 00:07:45,421
Very nice work, yeah.

173
00:07:45,508 --> 00:07:47,423
Can I talk to you
for a moment?

174
00:07:50,557 --> 00:07:51,819
[whispers] Quit that!

175
00:07:51,906 --> 00:07:53,951
- What?
- Poking the bigwig.

176
00:07:54,038 --> 00:07:55,736
"Poking the bigwig."

177
00:07:55,823 --> 00:07:59,043
Ah, I like it.
It's got a nice ring to it.

178
00:07:59,130 --> 00:08:02,482
I understand it's compulsion
you're not fully in control of

179
00:08:02,569 --> 00:08:04,179
but I wish you would try.

180
00:08:04,266 --> 00:08:07,269
It would make our work
a lot easier if you did...try.

181
00:08:07,356 --> 00:08:09,053
- Did you see that?
- What?

182
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:10,794
Right outside that window.

183
00:08:10,881 --> 00:08:13,536
There's a pink unicorn.
Amazing!

184
00:08:13,623 --> 00:08:15,277
Now you're hallucinating.

185
00:08:15,364 --> 00:08:18,106
No. No, no, look.
It's right outside the window.

186
00:08:18,193 --> 00:08:20,064
Seriously, look.
It's a unicorn!

187
00:08:20,151 --> 00:08:21,588
I want to see!

188
00:08:24,329 --> 00:08:26,506
- I don't see a unicorn.
- It's right there.

189
00:08:26,593 --> 00:08:28,203
[whispers]
I'm trying to trick her.

190
00:08:28,290 --> 00:08:30,553
Oh. Yes.

191
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:32,250
What a nice unicorn.

192
00:08:32,337 --> 00:08:33,774
'Yeah, very nice unicorn.'

193
00:08:33,861 --> 00:08:36,254
So you like to have
secret tea parties?

194
00:08:36,341 --> 00:08:38,082
How did you know
about the tea party?

195
00:08:38,169 --> 00:08:41,738
Oh, you know, I kind of like
secret tea parties, too.

196
00:08:41,825 --> 00:08:44,698
- I'm Patrick. That's Teresa.
- Hi.

197
00:08:44,785 --> 00:08:46,395
I'm Ashley Harrington.

198
00:08:46,482 --> 00:08:48,179
- It's a pleasure to meet you.
- You, too.

199
00:08:48,266 --> 00:08:49,790
Now who's your mom and dad?

200
00:08:49,877 --> 00:08:51,835
Xander Harrington
and Sadie Cardozo Harrington.

201
00:08:51,922 --> 00:08:53,794
I know my address
and phone number, too.

202
00:08:53,881 --> 00:08:57,319
Very good. Patrick and I are
actually looking for your mommy.

203
00:08:57,406 --> 00:08:58,973
- Do you know where she is?
- Outside.

204
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:00,714
We could go cheer her up.

205
00:09:00,801 --> 00:09:02,367
Is she sad, sweetie?

206
00:09:02,454 --> 00:09:05,327
She misses daddy.
He went away on a trip.

207
00:09:08,112 --> 00:09:09,505
'Yes, he did.'

208
00:09:12,116 --> 00:09:14,641
Mr. Harrington, you prodded
Heather Evans into going

209
00:09:14,728 --> 00:09:16,512
into your brother's office,
didn't you?

210
00:09:16,599 --> 00:09:18,514
I told her
the file was on the desk.

211
00:09:18,601 --> 00:09:20,951
What she did with that
information is her problem.

212
00:09:21,038 --> 00:09:23,127
You're the west coast
head of media operations

213
00:09:23,214 --> 00:09:26,043
and you tell an employee about
a confidential layoff list?

214
00:09:26,130 --> 00:09:28,742
Well, I had
a feeling she'd be grateful.

215
00:09:28,829 --> 00:09:30,831
And she was.

216
00:09:30,918 --> 00:09:32,920
You exchanged the information
for sex.

217
00:09:37,359 --> 00:09:39,448
Yes. I did.

218
00:09:39,535 --> 00:09:41,102
Gate security
has you leaving the party

219
00:09:41,189 --> 00:09:43,234
just before your brother died
of his injuries.

220
00:09:43,321 --> 00:09:46,194
I didn't know what had happened,
and I didn't particularly want

221
00:09:46,281 --> 00:09:49,501
to watch his coronation,
frankly. It's boring.

222
00:09:49,589 --> 00:09:52,809
Forgive me, Xander.

223
00:09:52,896 --> 00:09:54,637
Did you kill
your brother, Ed?

224
00:09:54,724 --> 00:09:56,465
Would you give us a second?

225
00:09:58,336 --> 00:09:59,990
No, I did not
kill my brother.

226
00:10:00,077 --> 00:10:01,905
Well, you didn't seem
that sad about his death.

227
00:10:01,992 --> 00:10:04,386
Well, maybe I just don't
display my emotions to cops.

228
00:10:05,735 --> 00:10:06,780
Maybe.

229
00:10:08,912 --> 00:10:10,479
Look, I'd like to tell you
that Xander and I

230
00:10:10,566 --> 00:10:12,350
were the best of friends,
but we weren't.

231
00:10:12,437 --> 00:10:15,789
We're just different people.
We weredifferent people.

232
00:10:18,574 --> 00:10:20,532
He'd..

233
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:23,579
...always do the right things,
say the right things.

234
00:10:23,666 --> 00:10:26,103
Dad would say, "jump."
he'd ask, "how high?"

235
00:10:26,190 --> 00:10:28,149
And you?

236
00:10:28,236 --> 00:10:30,630
Dad never asks me to jump.

237
00:10:30,717 --> 00:10:34,155
- Mommy! Mommy!
- Baby, hmm?

238
00:10:34,242 --> 00:10:36,418
- We're going to get ice cream.
- Okay. One scoop, okay?

239
00:10:36,505 --> 00:10:37,941
[kisses]
No spoiling dinner.

240
00:10:38,028 --> 00:10:39,987
- Okay.
- One scoop it is.

241
00:10:40,074 --> 00:10:41,902
And I'll e-mail
you the Arizona overnights.

242
00:10:41,989 --> 00:10:43,860
You've got that conference call
tomorrow with the affiliates.

243
00:10:43,947 --> 00:10:47,603
- Okay. Thanks, Elizabeth.
- Come on.

244
00:10:50,606 --> 00:10:52,695
You haven't told her?

245
00:10:52,782 --> 00:10:54,741
[sighs]

246
00:10:54,828 --> 00:10:57,918
How do you tell a six-year-old
her father's never coming home?

247
00:10:58,005 --> 00:10:59,397
Well, she pretty much
knows already.

248
00:10:59,484 --> 00:11:01,356
She just doesn't
understand it yet.

249
00:11:01,443 --> 00:11:05,055
Nor do I. I just..

250
00:11:05,142 --> 00:11:09,364
I keep thinking Xander would
know exactly what to say to her.

251
00:11:09,451 --> 00:11:10,974
It's so stupid, huh?

252
00:11:11,061 --> 00:11:12,889
Xander
must have had enemies..

253
00:11:12,976 --> 00:11:15,326
...business rivals
that resented his success?

254
00:11:15,413 --> 00:11:17,938
Um...sure.

255
00:11:18,025 --> 00:11:19,896
Yeah, but nothing abnormal.

256
00:11:21,681 --> 00:11:24,858
Well, there is
the "Visualize" thing.

257
00:11:24,945 --> 00:11:27,861
The "Observer" is about to run
an investigative series

258
00:11:27,948 --> 00:11:30,167
and it's gonna be
very critical.

259
00:11:30,254 --> 00:11:32,517
Visualize?
The sort of religious group?

260
00:11:32,604 --> 00:11:34,171
No, it's more like a cult.

261
00:11:34,258 --> 00:11:37,174
It's brainwashing
with a sideline in tax fraud.

262
00:11:37,261 --> 00:11:39,699
You know, we got
a-a pretty nasty letter

263
00:11:39,786 --> 00:11:41,526
from their leader,
Bret Stiles

264
00:11:41,613 --> 00:11:44,704
threatening unspecified
trouble if we ran it.

265
00:11:44,791 --> 00:11:46,227
I can get you a copy.

266
00:11:46,314 --> 00:11:48,708
Oh, you-you had me
at "cult."

267
00:11:52,102 --> 00:11:54,104
Creepy.

268
00:11:54,191 --> 00:11:56,454
What, you don't like
giant eyeballs?

269
00:11:56,541 --> 00:11:58,065
No.

270
00:11:59,936 --> 00:12:01,851
Hi. I'm Steven Wench.

271
00:12:01,938 --> 00:12:03,810
Most folks call me
Brother Steve.

272
00:12:03,897 --> 00:12:05,637
Are you the CBI folks
who called?

273
00:12:05,725 --> 00:12:08,815
Yeah. Brother Patrick
and, uh, my brother Cho.

274
00:12:08,902 --> 00:12:10,251
[Steve]
'Well, come this way.'

275
00:12:10,338 --> 00:12:11,905
'I'm sorry, but I'm afraid
Mr. Stiles'

276
00:12:11,992 --> 00:12:13,254
isn't on the premises today.

277
00:12:13,341 --> 00:12:14,516
Where is he?

278
00:12:14,603 --> 00:12:16,431
At this precise moment,
I don't know.

279
00:12:16,518 --> 00:12:18,389
Bret lives spontaneously.

280
00:12:18,476 --> 00:12:20,827
But I'm happy to help
with any questions.

281
00:12:20,914 --> 00:12:22,524
I have a question.

282
00:12:22,611 --> 00:12:25,222
Uh, what are they doing
in there with those gizmos?

283
00:12:25,309 --> 00:12:28,530
That's where our technicians
assess for inner vision.

284
00:12:28,617 --> 00:12:30,967
- Really?
- Mm-hmm.

285
00:12:31,054 --> 00:12:33,013
That sounds fun.
Can anyone do that?

286
00:12:33,100 --> 00:12:35,493
Um...sure.

287
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:37,452
Excellent. Cho, you got
the interview, right?

288
00:12:37,539 --> 00:12:39,062
Just, uh, sign in.

289
00:12:39,149 --> 00:12:40,411
Oh, yeah. Sure, sure.

290
00:12:40,498 --> 00:12:42,370
- Hey.
- Hi, I wanna go.

291
00:12:42,457 --> 00:12:44,633
- Have a seat.
- Cool.

292
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:46,635
Excellent.

293
00:12:46,722 --> 00:12:48,593
Alright.

294
00:12:48,680 --> 00:12:49,986
[imitates buzzer]

295
00:12:50,073 --> 00:12:51,292
Just kidding.

296
00:12:51,379 --> 00:12:53,076
W-what do I do?

297
00:12:53,163 --> 00:12:54,643
Well, place your fingers
on the orb

298
00:12:54,730 --> 00:12:55,862
and, uh, tell me your name.

299
00:12:55,949 --> 00:12:56,993
You first.

300
00:12:57,080 --> 00:12:58,473
David.

301
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,388
Hi, David. I'm Patrick.

302
00:13:00,475 --> 00:13:01,998
Fingers on the orb.

303
00:13:06,176 --> 00:13:08,962
'Do you find sadness
in the world around you?'

304
00:13:09,049 --> 00:13:11,312
Yes.

305
00:13:11,399 --> 00:13:14,837
Do you find yourself dwelling
on the misfortunes of your past?

306
00:13:16,230 --> 00:13:17,448
Yeah.

307
00:13:19,276 --> 00:13:21,191
Do you often think

308
00:13:21,278 --> 00:13:23,237
people are secretly
out to get you?

309
00:13:23,324 --> 00:13:24,934
Yes.

310
00:13:26,718 --> 00:13:28,459
How you doing?

311
00:13:28,546 --> 00:13:29,983
- How am I doing?
- Yeah.

312
00:13:30,070 --> 00:13:31,593
Uh, how am I doing?

313
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:33,682
Well, Patrick

314
00:13:33,769 --> 00:13:38,034
you have serious issues
with paranoia and depression

315
00:13:38,121 --> 00:13:40,863
and negative thinking
in general.

316
00:13:40,950 --> 00:13:42,778
What?

317
00:13:42,865 --> 00:13:46,695
I would recommend our immersive
visualization program for you

318
00:13:46,782 --> 00:13:48,828
as a matter of urgency.

319
00:13:51,352 --> 00:13:53,833
Oh, okay. Um..

320
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:56,661
do-do-do you..
Do you take that?

321
00:13:56,748 --> 00:13:58,881
Ah! Not so fast.

322
00:13:58,968 --> 00:14:00,709
You thought you had me,
didn't you?

323
00:14:04,887 --> 00:14:06,541
Maybe we should get going.

324
00:14:06,628 --> 00:14:08,151
We gotta go get a warrant.

325
00:14:16,681 --> 00:14:18,205
Before coming
to persecute us,

326
00:14:18,292 --> 00:14:21,208
did you even look
at Xander Harrington's wife?

327
00:14:21,295 --> 00:14:23,558
Sadie. We look at everyone, sir.
What about her?

328
00:14:23,645 --> 00:14:25,995
We heard there was trouble
in her marriage.

329
00:14:26,082 --> 00:14:30,652
If Xander divorced her,
there's a prenup.

330
00:14:30,739 --> 00:14:32,567
But if he dies,
she gets it all.

331
00:14:32,654 --> 00:14:34,047
Where'd you
hear that, Steve?

332
00:14:34,134 --> 00:14:36,092
Do-do you have someone
on the inside?

333
00:14:36,179 --> 00:14:38,181
I've said enough.
Please leave.

334
00:14:38,268 --> 00:14:39,574
Do you have a gift shop?

335
00:14:39,661 --> 00:14:41,271
'Cause I'd love
an eyeball mug or something.

336
00:14:41,358 --> 00:14:42,794
- No.
- Okay.

337
00:14:48,409 --> 00:14:50,237
[male #3]
Good job, Steven.

338
00:14:50,324 --> 00:14:54,545
Good job. That was...
beautifully handled.

339
00:14:54,632 --> 00:14:56,243
Thank you, Mr. Stiles.

340
00:14:56,330 --> 00:14:59,550
No, thank you.

341
00:14:59,637 --> 00:15:01,988
So the guy claims Stiles is only
threatening legal trouble

342
00:15:02,075 --> 00:15:04,468
that visualize is a religion
of science and peace.

343
00:15:04,555 --> 00:15:07,384
Tell that to the TV reporter
from Atlanta.

344
00:15:07,471 --> 00:15:09,865
Last spring,
he went off on visualize

345
00:15:09,952 --> 00:15:11,345
next day, he was flying
through the windshield

346
00:15:11,432 --> 00:15:13,042
of a member's car.

347
00:15:13,129 --> 00:15:15,740
One more thing. About Sadie
and Xander's marriage..

348
00:15:15,827 --> 00:15:17,438
An affair?

349
00:15:17,525 --> 00:15:19,222
No.

350
00:15:19,309 --> 00:15:22,399
Xander and I have been
partners in everything

351
00:15:22,486 --> 00:15:24,575
since the first week
of business school.

352
00:15:24,662 --> 00:15:27,752
I loved him,
and I respected him.

353
00:15:27,839 --> 00:15:29,972
I had no reason to stray.

354
00:15:30,059 --> 00:15:31,843
Neither did he.

355
00:15:31,931 --> 00:15:34,846
Sometimes you don't need
a reason.

356
00:15:34,934 --> 00:15:36,500
Oh.

357
00:15:36,587 --> 00:15:38,502
Agent Lisbon, you're here.

358
00:15:38,589 --> 00:15:40,113
Something to report?

359
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:42,202
No, she just, um, thinks

360
00:15:42,289 --> 00:15:44,639
I was screwing around
on your son.

361
00:15:44,726 --> 00:15:48,512
Excuse me.
I have better things to do.

362
00:15:48,599 --> 00:15:50,732
Do you think
she was having an affair?

363
00:15:50,819 --> 00:15:52,603
On balance, no.

364
00:15:53,648 --> 00:15:55,128
Go on.

365
00:15:55,215 --> 00:15:57,739
Sadie loves this company,
as well as my son.

366
00:15:57,826 --> 00:16:00,872
She'd never risk everything
for a roll in the hay.

367
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:03,484
Ambitious as all hell,
that girl.

368
00:16:03,571 --> 00:16:05,312
I like it.
Don't get me wrong.

369
00:16:05,399 --> 00:16:06,791
She's doubled our profit

370
00:16:06,878 --> 00:16:08,880
since she took over
running the TV stations.

371
00:16:08,968 --> 00:16:12,232
Um...
Excuse my rudeness.

372
00:16:12,319 --> 00:16:15,496
[beeps]

373
00:16:15,583 --> 00:16:16,888
Agent Lisbon.

374
00:16:20,370 --> 00:16:22,155
Get everybody
out of the house.

375
00:16:22,242 --> 00:16:24,070
- There's not enough time.
- Do what you can.

376
00:16:24,157 --> 00:16:26,028
Tara! Sadie!

377
00:16:26,115 --> 00:16:27,769
[cell phone rings]

378
00:16:27,856 --> 00:16:29,989
[groans]

379
00:16:30,076 --> 00:16:31,381
[beep]

380
00:16:31,468 --> 00:16:33,383
Hello?

381
00:16:33,470 --> 00:16:35,646
It's me. I need your help.

382
00:16:35,733 --> 00:16:37,257
Lisbon, you need my help?

383
00:16:37,344 --> 00:16:39,128
I need your help
finding a bomb.

384
00:16:45,308 --> 00:16:47,223
There's still time. You're good
at this kind of thing.

385
00:16:47,310 --> 00:16:48,877
If we find the bomb intact

386
00:16:48,964 --> 00:16:50,487
maybe we can still
get rid of it.

387
00:16:50,574 --> 00:16:52,620
What, are you nuts?
What do you want to do that for?

388
00:16:52,707 --> 00:16:54,535
It's a bomb.
Just get out of there.

389
00:16:54,622 --> 00:16:56,319
The guy's got comprehensive
home insurance, I'm sure.

390
00:16:56,406 --> 00:16:58,582
Oh, come on, Jane. We've got
two minutes. Alright?

391
00:16:58,669 --> 00:17:00,149
Where did they put it?

392
00:17:00,236 --> 00:17:02,369
They said it was somewhere
in the grand salon.

393
00:17:02,456 --> 00:17:03,979
Well, if they called it
the grand salon

394
00:17:04,066 --> 00:17:05,372
then obviously
they're insiders.

395
00:17:05,459 --> 00:17:06,634
Good point.

396
00:17:06,721 --> 00:17:08,331
Uh, l-leading us where?

397
00:17:08,418 --> 00:17:09,724
'Uh, let me see.'

398
00:17:09,811 --> 00:17:11,595
'Uh, if I was familiar
with that room'

399
00:17:11,682 --> 00:17:13,771
'I would plant a bomb..'

400
00:17:13,858 --> 00:17:17,166
'Try under the sofa.
No, no, no, no, no.'

401
00:17:17,253 --> 00:17:19,473
'The cupboard over by the Dutch
forgery in the corner.'

402
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:22,302
Uh, th-the painting?
How do you know it's a forgery?

403
00:17:22,389 --> 00:17:23,999
'Eh, the brushwork,
it's all wrong.'

404
00:17:24,086 --> 00:17:25,392
'It's way too loose.'

405
00:17:29,309 --> 00:17:32,921
- Oh, my God. You were right.
- 'Yeah, well, of course.'

406
00:17:33,008 --> 00:17:35,097
That looser feeling didn't
develop until much later on.

407
00:17:35,184 --> 00:17:38,057
No! The bomb. I found it.
One minute left.

408
00:17:38,144 --> 00:17:40,581
Oh, good. Time to leave.
Get out of there.

409
00:17:40,668 --> 00:17:42,409
Lisbon, if you think
I am even gonna engage

410
00:17:42,496 --> 00:17:44,063
in this game
of "which wire to pull"

411
00:17:44,150 --> 00:17:45,368
I'm not interested.
I don't wanna play that game.

412
00:17:45,455 --> 00:17:47,544
'Just leave right now.
Run. Okay?'

413
00:17:47,631 --> 00:17:50,808
- You're right. I'm outta here.
- Thank you. Good.

414
00:17:50,895 --> 00:17:53,985
Where's mommy?

415
00:17:54,073 --> 00:17:57,467
- Hang on.
- 'Ashley?'

416
00:17:57,554 --> 00:18:00,079
- Ashley, we have to leave now.
- No!

417
00:18:00,166 --> 00:18:01,602
'Lisbon!'

418
00:18:01,689 --> 00:18:03,212
Ashley, get out
from under there. Ashley.

419
00:18:03,299 --> 00:18:04,953
No! No! I want my mommy!

420
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:06,389
Whoa, whoa, whoa.
What's going on there?

421
00:18:06,476 --> 00:18:08,261
'Ashley, come on.'

422
00:18:08,348 --> 00:18:12,091
No! Leave me alone!
I want my mommy!

423
00:18:12,178 --> 00:18:13,962
Come out! You come out now!
That's an order!

424
00:18:14,049 --> 00:18:15,485
Lisbon, pick up
the girl and leave.

425
00:18:15,572 --> 00:18:17,008
Just pick her up
and take her outside.

426
00:18:17,096 --> 00:18:19,707
You can do that.
Run away now.

427
00:18:19,794 --> 00:18:21,100
Lisbon?

428
00:18:23,580 --> 00:18:24,842
Oh, God.
Oh, God.

429
00:18:24,929 --> 00:18:26,757
Lisbon!

430
00:18:26,844 --> 00:18:28,672
No, no, no, no.

431
00:18:28,759 --> 00:18:29,978
Screw it.

432
00:18:31,458 --> 00:18:32,589
Lisbon!

433
00:18:47,126 --> 00:18:49,563
You are a very bad girl.

434
00:18:49,650 --> 00:18:51,652
[sighs]
You're a bad girl.

435
00:18:53,262 --> 00:18:54,568
[sighs]

436
00:19:00,704 --> 00:19:02,793
Agent Lisbon.

437
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:04,360
Thank you.

438
00:19:04,447 --> 00:19:06,406
Thank you for saving
my granddaughter.

439
00:19:06,493 --> 00:19:08,234
We are so grateful.

440
00:19:08,321 --> 00:19:09,713
It's all part of the service.

441
00:19:09,800 --> 00:19:11,976
No, you were brave
and resourceful

442
00:19:12,063 --> 00:19:14,457
and I'll make sure
your supervisors say as much

443
00:19:14,544 --> 00:19:16,329
'in your file.'

444
00:19:16,416 --> 00:19:19,767
Look, if there's ever anything
I can do for you personally

445
00:19:19,854 --> 00:19:21,334
please don't hesitate.

446
00:19:21,421 --> 00:19:22,987
Well, you could offer her
the pad

447
00:19:23,074 --> 00:19:24,554
for a weekend during the summer.

448
00:19:24,641 --> 00:19:26,121
Actually, uh, I was wondering
if I could speak

449
00:19:26,208 --> 00:19:28,428
with some of your staff
who had access to that room.

450
00:19:28,515 --> 00:19:30,778
Ah, Edward.
Thank you for joining us.

451
00:19:30,865 --> 00:19:32,345
I just heard what happened.
Is everyone alright?

452
00:19:32,432 --> 00:19:34,173
Oh, everyone's fine. It's fine.

453
00:19:34,260 --> 00:19:35,652
Well, if there's anything I can
do to help

454
00:19:35,739 --> 00:19:37,132
just say the word. I'm here.

455
00:19:37,219 --> 00:19:40,266
Actually, uh, I'll be
issuing an announcement

456
00:19:40,353 --> 00:19:43,704
and I wanted to let
you and Sadie know first.

457
00:19:43,791 --> 00:19:46,185
Announcements?
Ooh, I love announcements.

458
00:19:46,272 --> 00:19:49,579
Um, sh-should we order up
a round of cocktails?

459
00:19:49,666 --> 00:19:51,233
I'll make this quick.

460
00:19:51,320 --> 00:19:53,931
Uh, as much as it pains me
to discuss business

461
00:19:54,018 --> 00:19:55,846
at a time like this..

462
00:19:55,933 --> 00:19:58,327
But the fact is,
we do have shareholders,

463
00:19:58,414 --> 00:20:01,896
and the board has demanded a new
C.E.O. be announced immediately.

464
00:20:01,983 --> 00:20:03,767
[whispers]
Sadie, I bet.

465
00:20:09,556 --> 00:20:11,819
- Uh..
- Sadie. Wow.

466
00:20:11,906 --> 00:20:14,517
'Uh... Ed, I-I'm sorry.'

467
00:20:14,604 --> 00:20:16,954
No. No, that's okay.

468
00:20:17,041 --> 00:20:19,696
That's.. It's your right
to name whoever you want.

469
00:20:19,783 --> 00:20:22,482
Uh, I-I don't know
what to say, Ed. I'm..

470
00:20:22,569 --> 00:20:26,181
No, it's okay. It's, um...
I get it. I get it.

471
00:20:26,268 --> 00:20:29,271
Um, I'm sure you'll do
a great job. Congratulations.

472
00:20:34,668 --> 00:20:36,713
Okay...

473
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:38,672
Well, I'm not gonna
say anything.

474
00:20:43,372 --> 00:20:45,113
The bomb threat..
We got something.

475
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:47,071
It came from the phone
of Elizabeth Stanfeld.

476
00:20:47,158 --> 00:20:51,162
Sadie Harrington's assistant.
Let's go get that warrant.

477
00:20:51,250 --> 00:20:52,555
Nice work.

478
00:20:52,642 --> 00:20:53,948
Thanks.

479
00:21:00,563 --> 00:21:03,218
Kitchen's clear.

480
00:21:03,305 --> 00:21:04,741
Bedroom, too.

481
00:21:07,701 --> 00:21:09,616
Van Pelt been
acting weird to you?

482
00:21:09,703 --> 00:21:12,401
Pleasedon't talk to me
about Van Pelt.

483
00:21:12,488 --> 00:21:14,925
No, I won't. Has she?

484
00:21:15,012 --> 00:21:17,276
No.

485
00:21:17,363 --> 00:21:19,800
It's just she's..
She's been smiling at me.

486
00:21:19,887 --> 00:21:21,802
No kidding.

487
00:21:21,889 --> 00:21:24,195
Sometimes I think
she might still be into me.

488
00:21:24,283 --> 00:21:26,154
That'd be weird, huh?

489
00:21:26,241 --> 00:21:28,417
You're weird.

490
00:21:28,504 --> 00:21:30,332
Cold.

491
00:21:30,419 --> 00:21:32,378
You know, I don't think
Stanfeld's been here in a while.

492
00:21:32,465 --> 00:21:34,902
Hey, check this out.

493
00:21:34,989 --> 00:21:37,078
"Visualize. A tool for living."

494
00:21:38,688 --> 00:21:41,517
'Elizabeth Stanfeld's
a member of visualize.'

495
00:21:41,604 --> 00:21:45,304
Looks like Bret Stiles is gonna
be talking to us after all.

496
00:21:45,391 --> 00:21:48,829
The eye is not
just the window to the soul.

497
00:21:48,916 --> 00:21:52,876
It is the key
to unlocking your greatness.

498
00:21:52,963 --> 00:21:54,661
It is the key

499
00:21:54,748 --> 00:21:58,273
because when you see it,
you will be it.

500
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:00,057
[Bret]
'One more time.'

501
00:22:03,147 --> 00:22:06,629
The eye is not just
the window to the soul.

502
00:22:06,716 --> 00:22:07,978
It is the key
to unlocking your greatness.

503
00:22:08,065 --> 00:22:10,807
[Patrick]
'Excuse me. I'm sorry. Uh..'

504
00:22:10,894 --> 00:22:15,116
The eye is the key?
Or the window? Or is it both?

505
00:22:15,203 --> 00:22:16,596
I'm a little confused.

506
00:22:16,683 --> 00:22:18,467
Mr. Jane, I presume.

507
00:22:18,554 --> 00:22:20,426
- Yeah.
- Hello. Welcome.

508
00:22:20,513 --> 00:22:22,297
Oh, you're expecting us?

509
00:22:22,384 --> 00:22:25,996
Oh, yes. And I presume this is
your long-suffering colleague

510
00:22:26,083 --> 00:22:27,563
agent Lisbon.

511
00:22:27,650 --> 00:22:31,088
If I may say, she is
more beautiful than advertised.

512
00:22:31,175 --> 00:22:33,700
Oh, thanks, guy.
Take a little break. Thank you.

513
00:22:33,787 --> 00:22:35,789
Give us a few minutes.
Thank you.

514
00:22:37,617 --> 00:22:39,053
[clears throat]

515
00:22:39,140 --> 00:22:40,794
Elizabeth Stanfeld..

516
00:22:40,881 --> 00:22:44,145
...your, um... "dear friend
and fine student"

517
00:22:44,232 --> 00:22:46,452
she planted a bomb
at the Harringtons' house

518
00:22:46,539 --> 00:22:49,063
'a bomb she couldn't have
possibly made herself.'

519
00:22:49,150 --> 00:22:52,022
Lisbon here defused it.
Heroine.

520
00:22:52,109 --> 00:22:56,157
Oh. Well, I-I'm..
I mean, I may have taught her.

521
00:22:56,244 --> 00:22:58,159
Uh... Stanfeld?

522
00:22:58,246 --> 00:22:59,856
Oh.

523
00:22:59,943 --> 00:23:01,771
Uh, w-was she a brunette?

524
00:23:01,858 --> 00:23:04,121
Mr. Stiles, "dear friend"?

525
00:23:04,208 --> 00:23:05,688
Well, yeah, but, you know

526
00:23:05,775 --> 00:23:07,037
I've signed millions
of these in my time.

527
00:23:07,124 --> 00:23:08,822
I always like
to personalize them.

528
00:23:08,909 --> 00:23:11,128
It's a little trick I learned
from an old friend of mine

529
00:23:11,215 --> 00:23:12,869
Ronnie Reagan.

530
00:23:12,956 --> 00:23:15,350
So you have no knowledge
of this woman?

531
00:23:15,437 --> 00:23:17,004
You have no knowledge
of any attacks

532
00:23:17,091 --> 00:23:20,790
on the Harrington family?
That's your position, is it?

533
00:23:20,877 --> 00:23:23,184
No, it's not my position.

534
00:23:23,271 --> 00:23:25,578
Actually, it's the truth.

535
00:23:25,665 --> 00:23:28,972
My eyes aren't, uh...
windows or doors

536
00:23:29,059 --> 00:23:30,409
or even keys,
for that matter.

537
00:23:30,496 --> 00:23:31,975
I use 'em
to look at things

538
00:23:32,062 --> 00:23:34,238
and what I'm looking at
right now is a big, fat liar.

539
00:23:34,325 --> 00:23:37,328
[chuckles]
Really?

540
00:23:37,416 --> 00:23:40,810
Really. Yes. Betrayed
by a faint facial tremor.

541
00:23:40,897 --> 00:23:42,856
You could work on that
in the mirror.

542
00:23:42,943 --> 00:23:46,033
You'd get
some great results.

543
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:47,687
Mm.

544
00:23:47,774 --> 00:23:50,559
You know, now I see why..

545
00:23:50,646 --> 00:23:54,563
...Red John really enjoys
sparring with you.

546
00:23:54,650 --> 00:23:57,131
A worthy adversary, yeah?

547
00:23:57,218 --> 00:23:58,437
Yes.

548
00:24:00,961 --> 00:24:03,485
Straight for the jugular.

549
00:24:03,572 --> 00:24:05,400
Obviously,
you struck a nerve.

550
00:24:05,487 --> 00:24:06,836
Hmm.

551
00:24:06,923 --> 00:24:10,274
Losing a wife and a child
like that..

552
00:24:10,361 --> 00:24:12,886
Well, it makes

553
00:24:12,973 --> 00:24:16,106
a man reckless, untethered.

554
00:24:16,193 --> 00:24:19,153
'Fills him with anger and shame'

555
00:24:19,240 --> 00:24:21,111
'that he cannot express.'

556
00:24:23,853 --> 00:24:27,204
Well, must makeyourjob
very hard, hmm?

557
00:24:27,291 --> 00:24:30,251
Yes, but the health
plan's great.

558
00:24:32,383 --> 00:24:34,429
[chuckles]

559
00:24:34,516 --> 00:24:37,171
Mr. Jane,
I think we can help you.

560
00:24:37,258 --> 00:24:38,955
I think we can scrub

561
00:24:39,042 --> 00:24:42,698
that shame and grief
and self-hatred

562
00:24:42,785 --> 00:24:45,222
right out of your system.

563
00:24:45,309 --> 00:24:47,224
Bret, please.

564
00:24:49,400 --> 00:24:51,794
[laughs]
We both know what you are.

565
00:24:51,881 --> 00:24:54,449
I mean, let's be honest here.
You're a con man.

566
00:24:54,536 --> 00:24:56,364
I mean, you're very charming,
and you're very good at it

567
00:24:56,451 --> 00:24:58,453
but you're just a con man.

568
00:24:58,540 --> 00:25:01,238
And you could no more help me
than you can fly to the moon.

569
00:25:01,325 --> 00:25:04,851
Mm.Nowwho's struck a nerve?

570
00:25:07,593 --> 00:25:10,334
Nobody's past helping..

571
00:25:10,421 --> 00:25:12,075
...even you.

572
00:25:12,162 --> 00:25:13,424
Believe me
when I say this, Bret

573
00:25:13,512 --> 00:25:14,904
you don't want
to make this personal.

574
00:25:16,732 --> 00:25:18,212
Now give us Stanfeld

575
00:25:18,299 --> 00:25:20,997
or I will make it
my sole purpose to hound you

576
00:25:21,084 --> 00:25:23,391
for the rest
of your miserable, phony life.

577
00:25:23,478 --> 00:25:25,915
[laughs]
Whoa.

578
00:25:26,002 --> 00:25:28,439
Am I hearing a threat here?

579
00:25:28,527 --> 00:25:31,399
I mean, a-aren't there rules
about this sort of thing? Hmm?

580
00:25:31,486 --> 00:25:33,357
Like you said, untethered.

581
00:25:34,968 --> 00:25:36,360
'Mr. Stiles'

582
00:25:36,447 --> 00:25:38,972
the percentage play here
is to give us Stanfeld.

583
00:25:39,059 --> 00:25:41,888
She's not gonna betray you.
She's loyal.

584
00:25:43,063 --> 00:25:45,152
Forget it, Lisbon.

585
00:25:45,239 --> 00:25:46,806
I'm bored.

586
00:25:50,287 --> 00:25:53,900
Elizabeth is at
Edward Harrington's penthouse.

587
00:25:53,987 --> 00:25:55,858
She went to Edward?
To do what?

588
00:25:55,945 --> 00:25:57,860
I don't know.

589
00:25:57,947 --> 00:26:01,168
Uh, she didn't tell me.
I gave her no instructions.

590
00:26:01,255 --> 00:26:04,388
She went of her
own free will.

591
00:26:04,475 --> 00:26:06,390
'Have a nice day.'

592
00:26:06,477 --> 00:26:08,915
Rigsby, get to Ed Harrington's
place immediately.

593
00:26:30,327 --> 00:26:32,329
[moans]

594
00:26:32,416 --> 00:26:34,070
Police! Don't move!

595
00:26:34,157 --> 00:26:36,246
- Let me see your hands! Freeze!
- Mr. Harrington?

596
00:26:36,333 --> 00:26:40,207
Mr. Harrington,
are you...alright?

597
00:26:40,294 --> 00:26:42,470
Yep. I think he's...fine.

598
00:26:42,557 --> 00:26:44,428
What the hell
is going on?

599
00:26:49,172 --> 00:26:50,696
[Rigsby]
'See, the way
it looks from here'

600
00:26:50,783 --> 00:26:53,046
'is that you and Elizabeth
were in this together.'

601
00:26:53,133 --> 00:26:54,874
Take out Xander

602
00:26:54,961 --> 00:26:56,832
take out your father

603
00:26:56,919 --> 00:26:59,400
live happily ever after
with her and Bret Stiles.

604
00:26:59,487 --> 00:27:03,143
No. I'm not into
that visualize crap.

605
00:27:03,230 --> 00:27:07,147
If I had any idea, I..
Uh, she was just..

606
00:27:07,234 --> 00:27:08,888
...just a friend.

607
00:27:08,975 --> 00:27:10,716
Convenient, though, that
she seems intent on taking out

608
00:27:10,803 --> 00:27:13,109
everyone between you and the top
job at Harrington media.

609
00:27:13,196 --> 00:27:14,241
Was Sadie next?

610
00:27:14,328 --> 00:27:16,156
How do you know
Iwasn't next?

611
00:27:16,243 --> 00:27:19,376
Visualize wanted to kill us all
because of that idiotic story.

612
00:27:19,463 --> 00:27:20,682
Then why didn't
she kill you, Ed?

613
00:27:20,769 --> 00:27:22,292
'I-I don't know.'

614
00:27:22,379 --> 00:27:23,946
- 'You ever meet Bret Stiles?'
- No.

615
00:27:24,033 --> 00:27:25,469
Anyone at all
from the visualize organization?

616
00:27:25,556 --> 00:27:26,645
No.

617
00:27:26,732 --> 00:27:28,429
So let me get this straight..

618
00:27:28,516 --> 00:27:30,300
she was sleeping with you
because you were a Harrington

619
00:27:30,387 --> 00:27:33,782
but not an important enough
Harrington to kill. Is that it?

620
00:27:33,869 --> 00:27:35,871
So, you're a stooge.

621
00:27:35,958 --> 00:27:39,005
Yeah, I'm a stooge.
Thanks for the heads-up.

622
00:27:46,142 --> 00:27:48,536
So Bret Stiles claims
you acted alone.

623
00:27:48,623 --> 00:27:50,538
But you had
an accomplice, didn't you?

624
00:27:50,625 --> 00:27:52,192
An accomplice to what?

625
00:27:52,279 --> 00:27:55,369
The bombing attempt.
We've got your phone records.

626
00:27:55,456 --> 00:27:57,240
Slam dunk. 20 years.

627
00:27:57,327 --> 00:28:01,375
2 decades.
7,000 days living in a box.

628
00:28:01,462 --> 00:28:02,724
'I don't know how people do it.'

629
00:28:02,811 --> 00:28:04,073
[Cho]
'Now help yourself out here.'

630
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:05,379
We know
someone gave you that bomb.

631
00:28:05,466 --> 00:28:07,076
We know
you didn't kill Xander.

632
00:28:07,163 --> 00:28:08,948
Sadie Harrington was with you
the entire party.

633
00:28:09,035 --> 00:28:12,342
- Of course I didn't kill him.
- But you know who did.

634
00:28:12,429 --> 00:28:14,693
Give us a name,
and we can talk a deal.

635
00:28:18,087 --> 00:28:20,960
I'm looking down at you
from a great height.

636
00:28:22,918 --> 00:28:25,138
'You're like ants.'

637
00:28:25,225 --> 00:28:28,445
It's sad how far
I am beyond you people..

638
00:28:28,532 --> 00:28:30,404
...spiritually and mentally.

639
00:28:30,491 --> 00:28:34,103
Mm. But, uh, physically

640
00:28:34,190 --> 00:28:36,715
I think we, uh,
we might have the upper hand.

641
00:28:36,802 --> 00:28:40,283
And physically is kinda
how they roll in prison.

642
00:28:40,370 --> 00:28:41,937
Just because
you've stopped my work

643
00:28:42,024 --> 00:28:44,244
doesn't mean others
won't follow in my path.

644
00:28:45,724 --> 00:28:47,682
'You can't imprison the truth.'

645
00:28:47,769 --> 00:28:51,642
Oh, Lizzy, you are
batnuts crazy. Batnuts, girl.

646
00:28:51,730 --> 00:28:54,863
But that's okay. You're young.
Youcanrecover your wits.

647
00:28:54,950 --> 00:28:56,604
'You're gonna have to work
very hard'

648
00:28:56,691 --> 00:28:58,345
but you've given me
a very good idea

649
00:28:58,432 --> 00:28:59,563
and I thank you.

650
00:29:01,217 --> 00:29:03,524
Excuse me. We've come
to take my son home.

651
00:29:03,611 --> 00:29:05,352
This is a family matter.

652
00:29:05,439 --> 00:29:07,789
I'm sorry, sir, but we haven't
finished questioning him.

653
00:29:07,876 --> 00:29:10,923
Edward made a mistake. It was
dangerous, yes, but not criminal

654
00:29:11,010 --> 00:29:14,361
so unless you have some evidence
linking him to a crime

655
00:29:14,448 --> 00:29:15,884
I'd like you to let him go.

656
00:29:15,971 --> 00:29:17,625
Let the poor wretch go,
Van Pelt.

657
00:29:17,712 --> 00:29:21,237
He's guilty of nothing except
extremely poor decision-making

658
00:29:21,324 --> 00:29:23,500
sexual partner-wise,
and, uh..

659
00:29:23,587 --> 00:29:25,415
...who hasn't been there?

660
00:29:25,502 --> 00:29:27,026
Jane.

661
00:29:27,113 --> 00:29:29,419
It isn't in my authority
to allow that, sir

662
00:29:29,506 --> 00:29:31,726
'but if you'll wait a moment,
I'll find agent Lisbon'

663
00:29:31,813 --> 00:29:33,815
'and she may be able
to help you out.'

664
00:29:33,902 --> 00:29:36,426
- 'Dad?'
- 'Never mind. There he is.'

665
00:29:36,513 --> 00:29:38,080
'Eddie, we're leaving.'

666
00:29:40,474 --> 00:29:43,999
You bastard!
You stupid son of a bitch!

667
00:29:44,086 --> 00:29:48,308
How could you endanger us?
Ashley was nearly killed.

668
00:29:48,395 --> 00:29:49,875
I'm sorry.

669
00:29:49,962 --> 00:29:51,528
I'm gonna go wait
in the car.

670
00:29:51,615 --> 00:29:53,139
I'm really sorry.

671
00:29:53,226 --> 00:29:55,315
You're sorry? Sorry?

672
00:29:56,620 --> 00:29:58,622
Have you
no common sense, boy?

673
00:29:58,709 --> 00:30:00,146
Are you completely--

674
00:30:00,233 --> 00:30:02,061
Sir, you're gonna have
to take it easy.

675
00:30:02,148 --> 00:30:06,065
Anyhow, here we are. Let's go
home and discuss this.

676
00:30:06,152 --> 00:30:09,024
[Van Pelt]
'Mr. Harrington, that
isn't going to be possible.'

677
00:30:09,111 --> 00:30:10,373
[ring]

678
00:30:10,460 --> 00:30:12,506
Oh, hell. Not again.

679
00:30:12,593 --> 00:30:14,813
- What's wrong?
- Miserable cowards.

680
00:30:14,900 --> 00:30:17,903
- More threats!
- What does it say?

681
00:30:17,990 --> 00:30:21,863
"Next time
you won't be so lucky."

682
00:30:21,950 --> 00:30:24,083
Sent from the same number
as the earlier threat.

683
00:30:24,170 --> 00:30:26,694
I thought you had traced
that call to Stanfeld.

684
00:30:26,781 --> 00:30:28,087
Well, they likely use
the same number

685
00:30:28,174 --> 00:30:29,610
for many different cloned cells.

686
00:30:29,697 --> 00:30:31,394
It proves it's
the same people, anyhow.

687
00:30:31,481 --> 00:30:32,961
Meaning Visualize.

688
00:30:33,048 --> 00:30:34,920
Agent Hightower,
I'm going to need

689
00:30:35,007 --> 00:30:36,617
additional protection
for my family

690
00:30:36,704 --> 00:30:38,662
at least until we can get
to the house in London.

691
00:30:38,749 --> 00:30:40,751
Mr. Harrington, while your
family's safety is at stake

692
00:30:40,839 --> 00:30:42,928
maybe it's best
to postpone the expose.

693
00:30:43,015 --> 00:30:44,930
If I gave in,
where does it stop?

694
00:30:45,017 --> 00:30:47,149
My family would be
under constant threat

695
00:30:47,236 --> 00:30:50,587
from people who don't like
what I print or air.

696
00:30:50,674 --> 00:30:52,546
We need protection,
Agent Hightower.

697
00:30:52,633 --> 00:30:54,243
Mr. Harrington, the police
assigned to you

698
00:30:54,330 --> 00:30:57,377
are very well-trained
personnel. We can add more if--

699
00:30:57,464 --> 00:31:00,510
I want your best people..
Ms. Lisbon and her team.

700
00:31:00,597 --> 00:31:02,251
I'll insist on it.

701
00:31:02,338 --> 00:31:04,036
Mr. Harrington, that's
very flattering, but you--

702
00:31:04,123 --> 00:31:07,604
And if you could release
my son now, I'd appreciate it.

703
00:31:10,129 --> 00:31:12,000
I'll see what we can do.

704
00:31:20,313 --> 00:31:22,619
She's about to throw out
the queen of spades.

705
00:31:22,706 --> 00:31:24,621
Stop showing off.

706
00:31:26,362 --> 00:31:28,843
[sighs]

707
00:31:28,930 --> 00:31:30,801
Ha.

708
00:31:30,889 --> 00:31:32,238
[Patrick]
'That's what's called'

709
00:31:32,325 --> 00:31:33,761
'cutting off your nose
to spite your face.'

710
00:31:33,848 --> 00:31:35,154
[cell phone rings]

711
00:31:35,241 --> 00:31:36,590
- I'll take that. Thank you.
- Lisbon.

712
00:31:36,677 --> 00:31:38,200
- Goodnight, everyone.
- Goodnight.

713
00:31:38,287 --> 00:31:40,986
Alex, I told Ash she could stay
up if you tucked her in.

714
00:31:41,073 --> 00:31:42,639
- Is that okay?
- Absolutely.

715
00:31:42,726 --> 00:31:43,902
Thank you.

716
00:31:46,426 --> 00:31:47,818
Night, sweetie.

717
00:31:47,906 --> 00:31:49,298
Perimeter's clear, boss.

718
00:31:49,385 --> 00:31:51,387
'Just checked in
with the guards outside.'

719
00:31:51,474 --> 00:31:55,391
Gin. There you go.
Who's next? Any takers?

720
00:32:05,184 --> 00:32:08,491
[clicks]

721
00:32:08,578 --> 00:32:10,450
- Wish me luck.
- Uh-huh.

722
00:32:18,414 --> 00:32:20,112
- Double.
- Sure.

723
00:32:22,157 --> 00:32:25,117
[clatter]

724
00:32:25,204 --> 00:32:26,292
'Mm.'

725
00:32:26,379 --> 00:32:27,510
You're a lucky man.

726
00:32:27,597 --> 00:32:29,556
- No such thing as luck.
- Really?

727
00:32:29,643 --> 00:32:32,689
It's all about knowing
the odds, seizing the chances.

728
00:32:32,776 --> 00:32:34,735
'No luck involved.'

729
00:32:34,822 --> 00:32:36,606
Hogwash.

730
00:32:36,693 --> 00:32:38,130
Hogwash?

731
00:32:38,217 --> 00:32:40,567
You heard me. Hogwash.
What's that?

732
00:32:42,699 --> 00:32:45,093
- What?
- Shh!

733
00:32:45,180 --> 00:32:46,312
[Lisbon]
'What are you..'

734
00:32:46,399 --> 00:32:48,183
[Patrick]
'Shh!'

735
00:32:48,270 --> 00:32:50,533
Wait a minute.
I hear it, too.

736
00:32:52,579 --> 00:32:54,450
[whispers]
I got Ashley covered here.

737
00:32:56,539 --> 00:32:58,150
[whispers]
It's coming from in there.

738
00:32:58,237 --> 00:32:59,499
[whispers]
Xander's office?

739
00:32:59,586 --> 00:33:01,022
It's been sealed
since the murder.

740
00:33:03,155 --> 00:33:05,200
[sighs]

741
00:33:15,776 --> 00:33:17,038
[switch clicks]

742
00:33:26,395 --> 00:33:27,788
Clear.

743
00:33:27,875 --> 00:33:29,920
Wow. Could've sworn
I heard someone in here.

744
00:33:33,011 --> 00:33:34,490
Oh, what's this?

745
00:33:36,362 --> 00:33:39,191
[chuckles]
That's Ashley's.

746
00:33:39,278 --> 00:33:41,889
- So?
- 'That's it.'

747
00:33:41,976 --> 00:33:43,760
What's it?

748
00:33:43,847 --> 00:33:47,329
She was here.
She was right here.

749
00:33:48,852 --> 00:33:50,724
- What's he talking about?
- No idea.

750
00:33:50,811 --> 00:33:52,552
That's why she's been
so quiet and withdrawn.

751
00:33:52,639 --> 00:33:54,119
She witnessed the murder.

752
00:33:56,469 --> 00:33:57,905
[alarm wailing]

753
00:33:57,992 --> 00:33:59,254
[Lisbon]
'The power's been cut.'

754
00:33:59,341 --> 00:34:03,911
Uh...w-we have
a-a backup generator.

755
00:34:03,998 --> 00:34:07,219
[Van Pelt]
'Perimeter's been breached.
Someone's on the grounds.'

756
00:34:07,306 --> 00:34:09,134
Copy that, Van Pelt.

757
00:34:09,221 --> 00:34:11,745
Charlie king one,
check the grounds.

758
00:34:11,832 --> 00:34:14,226
Charlie king one,
do you copy?

759
00:34:14,313 --> 00:34:16,184
'Charlie king one?'

760
00:34:20,754 --> 00:34:22,060
'We're on our way
to the safe room.'

761
00:34:22,147 --> 00:34:23,409
'Secure Tara, Sadie
and the baby. Go!'

762
00:34:26,455 --> 00:34:27,456
Come on.

763
00:34:28,936 --> 00:34:30,546
- Seal the door now.
- Mm.

764
00:34:33,375 --> 00:34:35,551
[lock clicks]

765
00:34:35,638 --> 00:34:37,162
[voice breaks]
Grandpa?

766
00:34:40,904 --> 00:34:42,732
[chuckles]

767
00:34:42,819 --> 00:34:44,647
It's okay, honey.

768
00:34:49,522 --> 00:34:50,740
Don't be scared.

769
00:34:52,525 --> 00:34:54,092
We're safe in here.

770
00:34:56,224 --> 00:34:58,357
Everything's gonna be
alright.

771
00:34:59,662 --> 00:35:02,665
Come on.

772
00:35:02,752 --> 00:35:05,059
There you go.

773
00:35:05,146 --> 00:35:07,496
Oh, I could do
with some Chocolate.

774
00:35:07,583 --> 00:35:11,239
You have any, uh,
chocolate, Grace?

775
00:35:11,326 --> 00:35:13,285
- No.
- Just a little bit?

776
00:35:13,372 --> 00:35:15,983
- Van Pelt, what's the status?
- Lisbon, that was very good.

777
00:35:16,070 --> 00:35:17,593
I have one question.
Who the hell is Charlie king?

778
00:35:17,680 --> 00:35:19,421
Call it up.

779
00:35:19,508 --> 00:35:21,075
[taps keys]

780
00:35:21,162 --> 00:35:23,077
[clicks]

781
00:35:26,167 --> 00:35:28,343
I want mommy.

782
00:35:31,390 --> 00:35:35,742
Hey, you remember the party
we had a few days ago?

783
00:35:35,829 --> 00:35:37,091
With all the flowers
and music?

784
00:35:37,178 --> 00:35:38,571
That's right.

785
00:35:38,658 --> 00:35:41,661
You were in daddy's office
that afternoon

786
00:35:41,748 --> 00:35:44,446
before the guests got here
for the party, remember?

787
00:35:44,533 --> 00:35:46,666
Uh, were you maybe..

788
00:35:46,753 --> 00:35:49,234
...hiding under his desk?

789
00:35:49,321 --> 00:35:52,019
Playing tea party?

790
00:35:52,106 --> 00:35:54,978
It's alright if you were.
You won't get in trouble.

791
00:35:58,068 --> 00:36:00,114
Tell grandpa
the truth, sweetheart.

792
00:36:03,422 --> 00:36:04,771
[sighs]

793
00:36:04,858 --> 00:36:07,252
Ashley...

794
00:36:07,339 --> 00:36:09,602
'if you were there'

795
00:36:09,689 --> 00:36:12,518
'maybe you saw the game'

796
00:36:12,605 --> 00:36:15,608
'grandpa was playing
with daddy.'

797
00:36:15,695 --> 00:36:17,175
Did you?

798
00:36:20,134 --> 00:36:22,092
What the hell is going on?

799
00:36:22,180 --> 00:36:24,138
Uh, we--it's a very clever plan.
Would you like me to explain?

800
00:36:24,225 --> 00:36:26,358
Wait. Why are they in the safe
room? What are you doing?

801
00:36:26,445 --> 00:36:28,229
Ma'am, if you'll come with me,
I'll explain everything.

802
00:36:28,316 --> 00:36:29,622
'I want to know.'

803
00:36:31,363 --> 00:36:33,191
Thirty seconds
and I'm pulling the plug.

804
00:36:34,757 --> 00:36:37,064
Daddy's dead, isn't he?

805
00:36:37,151 --> 00:36:38,544
It was a game.

806
00:36:39,980 --> 00:36:44,027
When your daddy fell.. I
t was an accident.

807
00:36:44,114 --> 00:36:46,334
I didn't mean to do it.

808
00:36:49,381 --> 00:36:51,209
[lowered voice]
Cho.

809
00:36:51,296 --> 00:36:53,472
Put your hands
on your head.

810
00:36:53,559 --> 00:36:54,864
Now.

811
00:36:56,475 --> 00:36:58,216
You're under arrest.

812
00:37:02,481 --> 00:37:03,786
[sighs]

813
00:37:07,790 --> 00:37:10,140
You're a bastard!

814
00:37:10,228 --> 00:37:12,360
Uh, well,
thanks for the, uh, feedback.

815
00:37:12,447 --> 00:37:13,753
Always appreciated.

816
00:37:13,840 --> 00:37:15,711
What's wrong with grandpa?

817
00:37:15,798 --> 00:37:18,018
It's okay, baby.
It's okay.

818
00:37:22,718 --> 00:37:24,459
You've got nothing.

819
00:37:24,546 --> 00:37:27,114
My lawyers will
grind this to dust.

820
00:37:28,985 --> 00:37:31,161
Don't say a single thing,
either of you.

821
00:37:31,249 --> 00:37:34,861
They're saying I killed Xan.

822
00:37:34,948 --> 00:37:36,297
Well, that's not true, right?

823
00:37:37,907 --> 00:37:39,344
Of course not.

824
00:37:39,431 --> 00:37:41,084
It was very sweet
seeing you with Ashley.

825
00:37:41,171 --> 00:37:43,826
Uh, I guess after two sons

826
00:37:43,913 --> 00:37:46,002
when she came along,
it lit up your world.

827
00:37:48,004 --> 00:37:49,223
Strange, though,
that you can't

828
00:37:49,310 --> 00:37:51,007
so much as look
at your own daughter

829
00:37:51,094 --> 00:37:53,445
nor barely touch
your lovely wife.

830
00:37:56,709 --> 00:37:59,538
How'd you find out
the baby wasn't yours?

831
00:37:59,625 --> 00:38:01,975
I know that you believed Tara
was having an affair with Xander

832
00:38:02,062 --> 00:38:04,194
and that your son was
Ella's biological father.

833
00:38:04,282 --> 00:38:05,848
'And you were partly right.'

834
00:38:05,935 --> 00:38:07,241
'I mean, she was having an
affair with your son'

835
00:38:07,328 --> 00:38:10,592
but it wasn't with Xander.
Was it, Ed?

836
00:38:10,679 --> 00:38:12,377
Huh?Oh!

837
00:38:12,464 --> 00:38:14,335
Yeah, I figured it out
when I saw you two at the CBI.

838
00:38:14,422 --> 00:38:16,859
She was very angry
with you, very angry.

839
00:38:16,946 --> 00:38:18,470
'Pure jealousy.'

840
00:38:20,254 --> 00:38:21,560
Oh, my God.

841
00:38:22,474 --> 00:38:24,040
I'm..

842
00:38:24,127 --> 00:38:25,477
Ella's father.
Congratulations.

843
00:38:25,564 --> 00:38:27,696
She's a real peach, actually.
She's great.

844
00:38:27,783 --> 00:38:30,873
No. Can't be.

845
00:38:30,960 --> 00:38:32,701
[whispers]
I am so sorry.

846
00:38:32,788 --> 00:38:35,008
No! It was Xander!

847
00:38:37,837 --> 00:38:39,186
It was Xander.

848
00:38:39,273 --> 00:38:40,883
Okay. We'll take that
as a confession.

849
00:38:40,970 --> 00:38:43,103
- Thank you.
- Come on. Let's go.

850
00:38:43,190 --> 00:38:45,453
You really don't think
I can do anything, do you?

851
00:38:47,803 --> 00:38:50,458
- It can't be.
- Well, it is. Sorry.

852
00:38:51,590 --> 00:38:53,113
You killed the wrong son.

853
00:38:54,854 --> 00:38:56,377
[gasps]

854
00:39:02,905 --> 00:39:06,779
Xander and Tara
seemed too close.

855
00:39:08,911 --> 00:39:11,827
Always whispering together
in the corners

856
00:39:11,914 --> 00:39:14,743
breaking apart
when I'd come into the room.

857
00:39:14,830 --> 00:39:17,093
They were friends.

858
00:39:17,180 --> 00:39:19,400
They were probably talking
about her affair with Ed.

859
00:39:23,143 --> 00:39:27,103
So I ordered
a paternity test.

860
00:39:27,190 --> 00:39:28,627
The results came back

861
00:39:28,714 --> 00:39:30,585
the day of the party.

862
00:39:33,414 --> 00:39:36,417
Ella is not my child..

863
00:39:36,504 --> 00:39:38,637
...but you know
that already, don't you?

864
00:39:38,724 --> 00:39:40,378
What did you expect, dad?

865
00:39:40,465 --> 00:39:42,554
You treat her like just another
one of your possessions

866
00:39:42,641 --> 00:39:43,816
but she's not!

867
00:39:43,903 --> 00:39:45,687
[Alex]
'I confronted Xan.'

868
00:39:45,774 --> 00:39:48,081
- You betrayed me!
- He didn't deny it.

869
00:39:50,997 --> 00:39:54,087
Why didn't he just deny it?

870
00:39:54,174 --> 00:39:56,872
[Patrick]
'He just naturally assumed
you knew it was Ed.'

871
00:39:56,959 --> 00:39:59,048
You!

872
00:39:59,135 --> 00:40:00,746
Aah!

873
00:40:00,833 --> 00:40:02,748
When I think..

874
00:40:04,271 --> 00:40:07,840
...of what poor Ashley saw..

875
00:40:07,927 --> 00:40:10,059
...she didn't see anything.
I made it up.

876
00:40:12,801 --> 00:40:14,281
But the teacup,
she was there.

877
00:40:14,368 --> 00:40:16,849
[whispers]
Yes, I know.

878
00:40:16,936 --> 00:40:19,417
But it's all in the wrist.

879
00:40:34,649 --> 00:40:36,477
[Bret]
'My good friend Senator Aleman'

880
00:40:36,564 --> 00:40:38,610
is very concerned
about my treatment.

881
00:40:38,697 --> 00:40:41,134
Of course, youdoknow
the good senator, huh?

882
00:40:41,221 --> 00:40:43,136
Chair of
the justice committee.

883
00:40:43,223 --> 00:40:45,138
I know him.

884
00:40:45,225 --> 00:40:47,880
Bret Stiles.
Good to see you.

885
00:40:47,967 --> 00:40:49,447
Looking sharp.

886
00:40:49,534 --> 00:40:51,753
[clears throat]
I mean, I'd hate to tell him

887
00:40:51,840 --> 00:40:55,975
that you failed to resolve
this matter.

888
00:40:56,062 --> 00:40:57,716
So why don't we
resolve it right now?

889
00:40:57,803 --> 00:40:59,413
- Mm.
- Good.

890
00:40:59,500 --> 00:41:01,981
Patrick, Mr. Stiles
here says that

891
00:41:02,068 --> 00:41:05,114
you antagonized him
and conducted yourself

892
00:41:05,201 --> 00:41:07,116
in a way that reflected
poorly on the bureau.

893
00:41:07,203 --> 00:41:08,596
Do you have anything
to say?

894
00:41:10,468 --> 00:41:13,166
Uh, no.
I'm-I'm good, thanks.

895
00:41:13,253 --> 00:41:14,776
Okay.

896
00:41:14,863 --> 00:41:16,256
Actually, there's one thing
I'd like to say.

897
00:41:16,343 --> 00:41:17,866
Is this gonna be helpful,
Patrick?

898
00:41:17,953 --> 00:41:19,564
- Probably not.
- Okay, then, there you go.

899
00:41:19,651 --> 00:41:21,957
Nice talking with you.

900
00:41:22,044 --> 00:41:24,917
What? You're-you're kidding,
right? That's it?

901
00:41:25,004 --> 00:41:27,963
No. Do give my best
to Senator Aleman.

902
00:41:28,050 --> 00:41:30,400
Is he still seeing
that stripper in Bakersfield?

903
00:41:32,054 --> 00:41:33,665
'Lovely girl.'

904
00:41:37,451 --> 00:41:38,974
Well, well.

905
00:41:41,107 --> 00:41:43,936
I can see that
I've met my match here.

906
00:41:44,023 --> 00:41:45,720
You two..

907
00:41:45,807 --> 00:41:47,592
...quite the double act.

908
00:41:47,679 --> 00:41:50,464
Ah. I will retreat,
chastened.

909
00:41:54,120 --> 00:41:56,035
By the way..

910
00:41:58,559 --> 00:42:01,431
...we will find a way to charge
you with that attempted bombing.

911
00:42:03,259 --> 00:42:05,523
Miss Hightower, shh.

912
00:42:05,610 --> 00:42:07,525
Don't push your luck.

913
00:42:11,311 --> 00:42:12,834
So long, Bret.

914
00:42:14,967 --> 00:42:17,709
Don't draw the
wrong lesson here, Patrick.

915
00:42:17,796 --> 00:42:19,493
I've got Stiles checkmated.

916
00:42:19,580 --> 00:42:22,061
But if a player
with real juice came in here

917
00:42:22,148 --> 00:42:25,412
I'd want to see
you dance for him.

918
00:42:25,499 --> 00:42:27,588
Isn't that what we just did?

919
00:42:27,675 --> 00:42:29,503
You know what I mean.

920
00:42:29,590 --> 00:42:31,244
Okay.

921
00:42:31,331 --> 00:42:33,115
And I do love to dance.

922
00:42:46,259 --> 00:42:48,740
Stay tuned for scenes
from our next episode.

